You are the implementing engineer for Atlas ERP, in '/Users/taha/Claude projects/expremental ERP/atlas-erp' on branch feat/core-docflow (dev merged). NO git commands. uv at ~/.local/bin/uv; run `cd backend && ~/.local/bin/uv run <cmd>`.

READ FIRST (binding): CLAUDE.md; STRUCTURE.md (§2 core file list includes docflow.py + numbering.py; §7 naming, API route conventions, 400-line cap); docs/architecture.md section "D-012 — Document registry, flow links, and gapless numbering (merged)" COMPLETELY (normative — implement EXACTLY), plus skim D-007 (tenancy), D-010 (audit), D-011 (run_in_uow / publish — numbering is claimed in the transaction that makes a document permanent), D-014 (error envelope), D-025 (tests). Then read existing code: backend/app/core/{models.py,db.py,tenancy.py,events.py,deps.py,exceptions.py,schemas.py}, backend/app/main.py, backend/alembic/versions/0005_audit.py (latest=0005), backend/tests/conftest.py.

TASK — PLAN 3.7 (+ numbering half of 3.8): document registry, flow links, and gapless per-tenant numbering, per D-012. Deliverables:

1. `backend/app/core/numbering.py`:
   - `core_number_sequences` model (table `core_number_sequences`): UuidPKMixin, TenantMixin, TimestampMixin; columns: name (str — the sequence key, e.g. "finance.invoice"), prefix (str, e.g. "INV"), padding (int, e.g. 5), next_value (int, default 1, not null), year_reset (bool — whether the running number resets per year), current_year (int nullable — the year the counter belongs to when year_reset). UNIQUE(tenant_id, name). composite tenant_fk to adm_tenants.
   - `claim_number(session, tenant_id, sequence_name, *, on_date) -> str` per D-012: atomic increment via an `UPDATE core_number_sequences SET next_value = next_value + 1 WHERE tenant_id=:t AND name=:n RETURNING next_value` (UPDATE ... RETURNING works on Postgres AND modern SQLite — verify on the project's aiosqlite version; if RETURNING isn't supported there, do a portable SELECT...FOR UPDATE-then-UPDATE inside the caller's transaction, but PREFER RETURNING and TEST it works on SQLite). Format as `{prefix}-{year?}-{padded}` e.g. INV-2026-00001 (year segment present when year_reset). Year handling: if year_reset and on_date.year != current_year, reset next_value to 1 and set current_year before claiming (do this atomically/safely). The claim happens INSIDE the business transaction (D-012: numbers are claimed only in the transaction that makes a document permanent — so a rolled-back transaction returns the number to... no: gapless-or-not — D-012 specifies the exact policy; FOLLOW IT. Re-read D-012's gaplessness stance: it says gaplessness falls out of ACID because numbers are claimed only at the moment of permanence, drafts numbered at posting. So a number is claimed in the same transaction that commits the document; if that transaction rolls back, the increment rolls back too — sequential, effectively gapless for committed docs. Implement exactly that.)
   - `ensure_sequence(session, tenant_id, name, prefix, padding, year_reset)` — idempotent creator used by provisioning/seed (under system_context or tenant context).
2. `backend/app/core/docflow.py` + models:
   - `core_documents` registry (table `core_documents`): UuidPKMixin, TenantMixin, TimestampMixin; columns: doc_type (str — e.g. "sales.order", "finance.journal_entry"), doc_id (uuid — the business row's PK in its own table), doc_number (str nullable — assigned at posting), status (str nullable). UNIQUE(tenant_id, doc_type, doc_id). A partial unique index on (tenant_id, doc_number) WHERE doc_number IS NOT NULL (per D-012 — portable: Postgres partial index; SQLite supports partial indexes too — use sqlalchemy Index with sqlite_where AND postgresql_where, per D-021's dialect-kwargs pattern). 
   - `DocumentMixin` — a mixin business models use so each business document row links to its registry entry: per D-012 the business row carries a NOT NULL document_id FK to core_documents. (Design the API: a model mixes in DocumentMixin → gets document_id column + helper; OR the service registers the document and stores the link. Choose the cleaner approach that matches D-012's "DocumentMixin NOT NULL document_id" text and keep it usable by later modules. Provide a `register_document(session, tenant_id, doc_type, doc_id, ...)` service that inserts the registry row and returns it.)
   - `core_doc_links` (table `core_doc_links`): UuidPKMixin, TenantMixin, TimestampMixin; predecessor_document_id (FK to core_documents), successor_document_id (FK to core_documents), link_type (str nullable, e.g. "fulfills","invoices"); UNIQUE(tenant_id, predecessor_document_id, successor_document_id). composite tenant_fks.
   - `link_documents(session, tenant_id, predecessor, successor, link_type)` service.
   - `get_document_chain(session, tenant_id, document_id) -> chain` — traverse BOTH directions (ancestors + descendants) via ONE recursive CTE per D-012 (a recursive CTE that works on Postgres and SQLite — both support WITH RECURSIVE). Return a structure usable by an API: nodes (documents with type/number/status) + edges (predecessor→successor with link_type). Guard against cycles in traversal.
3. Migration `backend/alembic/versions/0006_docflow_numbering.py` (revision 0006, down_revision 0005): create core_number_sequences, core_documents (+ partial unique index), core_doc_links. Portable/batch-safe DDL; the partial index via the dialect-kwargs pattern.
4. A small read API for the document chain (D-012 says the UI renders the chain): a core router `core/docflow_router.py`? Per the auth precedent (D-027 put a core router in core/security_router.py), add `core/docflow_router.py` with GET `/api/v1/documents/{document_id}/chain` returning the chain (guarded by get_current_user; tenant-scoped). Wire into create_app(). Add schemas (DocumentRead, DocChainNode, DocChainEdge, DocChainResponse) in core/schemas.py or beside the router (match the auth-schema precedent — note your choice). Record this core-router addition like D-027.
5. Tests `backend/tests/core/test_numbering.py` and `backend/tests/core/test_docflow.py`:
   Numbering:
   - sequential claim returns INV-2026-00001, -00002, ... (padding + prefix + year);
   - year_reset: claiming in a new year resets to 00001 with the new year segment;
   - concurrency/atomicity: two claims never return the same number (simulate sequential claims in one tenant; and assert the UPDATE...RETURNING path is atomic — if you can, a test with two sessions both claiming proves uniqueness; at minimum prove no duplicates across many claims);
   - rollback returns the number to the pool effectively: claim inside a transaction that rolls back → next committed claim reuses that value (gaplessness for committed docs per D-012) — assert the number after a rolled-back claim is the same as it would have been;
   - tenant isolation: two tenants have independent counters with the same sequence name;
   - ensure_sequence idempotent.
   Docflow:
   - register_document creates a registry row (unique per doc_type+doc_id);
   - link_documents creates an edge; duplicate edge rejected;
   - get_document_chain returns the full chain across 3+ linked docs in BOTH directions from a middle node (e.g. requisition→PO→GR→invoice, query from PO returns all four);
   - chain traversal handles a diamond / shared predecessor without infinite loop;
   - partial unique index: two documents can both have NULL doc_number, but two with the SAME doc_number in one tenant conflict (assert IntegrityError); different tenants may share a doc_number;
   - tenant isolation of the chain (tenant A's chain query never returns tenant B docs);
   - the /api/v1/documents/{id}/chain endpoint returns the chain for an authed user (use authed_client) and 404/empty for unknown id.
   Add conftest helpers as needed (a fixture to create documents/sequences under a tenant).

RULES: no TODO/stub/placeholder; absolute imports; ruff + pytest FULLY green; delete stray atlas.db; files <400 lines (split models if needed but prefer core/docflow.py holding logic + models in core/models.py — actually D-012 entities can live in their concern file; keep models.py from bloating: you MAY define the numbering/docflow ORM models inside numbering.py/docflow.py respectively if that's cleaner and keeps models.py lean — choose and note it, consistent with how auth put User in models.py; for consistency PREFER models in core/models.py unless it pushes it over ~350 lines, in which case put them in their concern file and note it); comments only for non-obvious constraints; don't print file contents in final reply. CRITICAL: verify UPDATE...RETURNING and WITH RECURSIVE actually work on the project's aiosqlite — if a test reveals they don't, use the portable fallback and document it.

Return: files created/changed, test counts (total + new), the claim_number/get_document_chain signatures, whether UPDATE...RETURNING + WITH RECURSIVE worked on SQLite (y/n, and fallback if used), the new core router recorded, and every deviation with one-line justification. Note any D-012 element deferred and to which PLAN task.

